  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14189987/assets/661520d112c7c7ef1c4591c3708f28fdb900d9ae6d036f774593239318205e09.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?",
  "worked_solution": "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 2.$ Note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-2.$ Similarly\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=2.$\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote.",
